                <h2>1. Why Do Multi-Account Teams Need Tiered Management?</h2>
<p>Many teams start experiencing chaos when managing more than 10 accounts. A typical scenario: an e-commerce team runs 20 ad accounts simultaneously across Facebook, Google, and TikTok. During product launch seasons, all accounts run at full capacity, the creative team is overwhelmed, and optimization specialists are exhausted. The result: budget is spent, but results are inconsistent, and nobody knows which account is the real growth engine.</p>
<p>The essence of tiered management is <strong>resource allocation efficiency</strong>. Not every account deserves equal attention, and not every account issue requires immediate intervention. After tiering, teams can quickly identify which accounts need intervention and which can run on autopilot.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Core principle: An account's value isn't determined by how much budget it spends, but by its contribution to business goals.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>2. What Are Common Standards for Account Tiering?</h2>
<p>In practice, account tiering typically considers three dimensions: <strong>business contribution, risk exposure, and maintenance cost</strong>.</p>
<h3>1. Tiering by Business Contribution</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Core accounts</strong>: Main product accounts contributing 80% of revenue, with high budgets and clear ROI targets</li>
<li><strong>Test accounts</strong>: Experimental accounts for new product testing and creative exploration, with acceptable room for trial and error</li>
<li><strong>Long-tail accounts</strong>: Stable accounts with limited contribution, small budgets, no need for refined operations</li>
</ul>
<h3>2. Tiering by Risk Level</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>High-risk accounts</strong>: Sensitive categories, frequent complaints, or historical violations</li>
<li><strong>Normal accounts</strong>: Stable delivery with no compliance issues</li>
<li><strong>Probation accounts</strong>: Newly opened or recently unbanned, requiring close monitoring</li>
</ul>
<h3>3. Tiering by Maintenance Cost</h3>
<p>High-budget accounts don't necessarily mean high maintenance costs. An account burning $5,000 daily with stable creative and conversion data might require less maintenance than a $500-daily account that needs daily bid adjustments.</p>
<hr />
<h2>3. How to Allocate Budget and Personnel for Different Tiers?</h2>
<p>After tiering, resource allocation logic must change accordingly.</p>
<p><strong>Core accounts</strong> should have dedicated optimization specialists. Budget is sufficient and stable, with the goal of achieving stable ROI and scaling. Optimization frequency can be 1-2 times daily without major adjustments.</p>
<p><strong>Test accounts</strong> can be managed by experienced optimization specialists on a part-time basis. Budget is limited but creative flexibility is high, with the goal of quickly validating creative directions and audience hypotheses. Higher optimization frequency is acceptable, but individual adjustment ranges should be controlled.</p>
<p><strong>Long-tail accounts</strong> can run basic operations through automated rules. Personnel only need periodic data report checks, no need for daily monitoring.</p>
<hr />
<h2>4. How to Monitor and Adjust After Tiering?</h2>
<p>Tiering isn't a one-time task; it requires dynamic adjustment. The following scenarios trigger tier reassessment:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Core account ROI drops below baseline by 20% for 3 consecutive days</strong> → Demote to probation tier</li>
<li><strong>Test account single creative CTR exceeds average by 3x</strong> → Consider upgrading or adding budget</li>
<li><strong>Long-tail account spend drops below expected by 50% for one week</strong> → Evaluate for shutdown or consolidation</li>
</ol>
<p>Weekly account health reviews are recommended, with tier adjustments incorporated into standard processes rather than scrambling when problems arise.</p>
<hr />
<h2>5. Common Problems in Tiered Management</h2>
<h3>Problem 1: Tiering Standards Are Too Subjective</h3>
<p>If tiering standards are decided arbitrarily by management, execution teams will struggle to follow through. Quantifiable standards are recommended—e.g., "core accounts are defined as those with monthly spend exceeding $X and ROI exceeding $Y."</p>
<h3>Problem 2: Tiers Become Rigid Without Dynamic Adjustments</h3>
<p>Some teams categorize accounts into ABC tiers and leave them unchanged for half a year. Markets and products change, and account performance fluctuates. Tier status should be evaluated at least monthly.</p>
<h3>Problem 3: Unclear Responsibilities After Tiering</h3>
<p>Post-tiering, disputes often arise over "who owns this account." It's recommended that every account, whether core or test, has a designated owner responsible for its performance.</p>
<hr />
<h2>6. How to Build a Sustainable Tiered Management System?</h2>
<p>Building a sustainable tiered management system relies on three elements: <strong>standardization, documentation, and automation tools</strong>.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Develop tiering SOPs</strong>: Define which metrics trigger tier adjustments, who has adjustment authority, and when reviews occur after adjustments</li>
<li><strong>Build account dashboards</strong>: Use tools to aggregate key data across all accounts, giving teams immediate visibility into account status</li>
<li><strong>Set alert rules</strong>: Automatically notify when core accounts show budget anomalies, bid spikes, or conversion drops</li>
<li><strong>Schedule regular reviews</strong>: Reassess tiering standards quarterly to eliminate outdated dimensions</li>
</ol>
<p>The ultimate goal of tiered management is to focus team attention on the right things. Multi-account advertising isn't scary—what's scary is applying equal effort to all accounts, resulting in important matters being poorly managed while unimportant matters waste resources.</p>
<p><strong>Direct judgment</strong>: If your team struggles with constant switching between multiple accounts daily, investing 1-2 days to establish an account tiering plan is the highest-ROI time investment in the long run.</p>
